I cannot think of a good way to approach this, the current option is to explicitly check for this [here](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/main/x-pack/plugin/transform/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/transform/transforms/ClientTransformIndexer.java#L217):
```java
 else if (irrecoverableException instanceof IndexNotFoundException && IndexerState.ABORTING == getState()) {
    logger.debug(
        "[{}] Bulk index experienced IndexNotFoundException failure while Transform is aborting. This is likely "
            + "due to the Transform delete API called with delete_dest_index=true.  Aborting indexer. ",
        getJobId()
    );
    onAbort();
    // do not call listener
}
```

This is similar to what happens [before](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/main/x-pack/plugin/core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/indexing/AsyncTwoPhaseIndexer.java#L483) and [after](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/main/x-pack/plugin/core/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/core/indexing/AsyncTwoPhaseIndexer.java#L573) this code is invoked, where the Transform exits gracefully if it was moved into the `ABORTING` state (via the `DELETE` API).

The alternative is to have [fail](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/main/x-pack/plugin/transform/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/transform/transforms/TransformTask.java#L506) check for the `ABORTING` state and exit gracefully there, but that is a more invasive change with broader impact
